Polyfill missing Node-18-only globals in bootstrap-fork.ts's own process,
before it loads anything else -- this embedded nodejs-mobile runtime is
Node 18.20.4, which is missing two globals vscode's own code and its
bundled dependencies reference unconditionally at module-load time:

- `File`: a default global only since Node 20 (Node 18 exposes it only
  as a named export of node:buffer, since 18.13.0).
- `crypto` (Web Crypto API): a default global only since Node 19 (Node
  18 exposes the exact same interface -- confirmed against Node's own
  v18.20.4 source, lib/internal/crypto/webcrypto.js, which has both
  randomUUID and getRandomValues -- only as node:crypto's named
  `webcrypto` export).

Both were found the same way, one after the other, via a real captured
node-stdio.log from an actual Simulator run -- not guessed:
- `File`: vscode's bundled `undici` dependency (pulled in transitively
  by the extension host's own HTTP client wiring, e.g.
  @vscode/proxy-agent) references the global `File` unconditionally at
  module-load time (node_modules/undici/lib/web/webidl/index.js),
  crashing every single extension host connection attempt with
  "ReferenceError: File is not defined" / "Extension Host Worker exited
  with code: 1".
- `crypto`: once the `File` fix let the extension host get further,
  the *next* connection attempt crashed instead with "ReferenceError:
  crypto is not defined", stack-traced into
  vs/workbench/api/node/extensionHostProcess.js -- vscode's own
  vs/base/common/uuid.ts references the bare `crypto` global
  unconditionally in generateUuid's module-load-time IIFE (`typeof
  crypto.randomUUID === 'function'`), and that module is pulled in
  early enough in the extension host's own bootstrap chain to hit this
  before anything else runs.

Both guarded (`typeof globalThis.X === 'undefined'`) so they're a no-op
on any Node build where the global already exists (Node 20+/19+, or a
real Electron/desktop build) -- this only ever does anything on this
specific embedded, older-Node mobile runtime.

Verified the same way every other patch in this project is: the
intended edits were applied to a real copy of the pinned vscode
commit's source (08d4889f9ec4a1685d257b9b95de036c8e1ce1e5) and diffed
programmatically (rather than hand-writing hunk headers), then
confirmed to apply with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` (zero tolerance for
near-miss context) with the resulting file byte-identical to the
intended target. Not run through `tsc` for real (no local vscode build
environment in this pipeline) -- code-server's own Build CI (which does
build vscode for real) is the actual verification for that.

